Intercom product decisions and first vertical slice

Workstream ID: ic-02
GitHub issue: https://github.com/Zanark/Intercom/issues/2
Status: IN_PROGRESS
Milestone: An explicit implementable first-slice contract
Dependencies: ic-01

Purpose:
  Turn the stated desktop/local-agent direction into a bounded implementation
  contract without assuming features, permissions, providers, or a framework.

Tasks:
  [x] Define the first user workflow, inputs, outputs, and exclusions.
  [ ] Select initial OS, desktop stack, and supported local-agent interface.
  [ ] Define data access, storage, network use, confirmations, and cancellation.
  [x] Record proposed success/failure scenarios and qualification criteria.
  [x] Record gated future workstreams for Agency, speech, desktop and reliability.
  [x] Record performance/speed as top priority/default and selectable priorities.
  [x] Record deep primary-source technology research and explicit reversal gates.
  [x] Define runtime/IPC/job/recovery contracts and dependency-driven packages.
  [ ] Finalize resource thresholds and decisions from ic-04/ic-05 receipts.
  [x] Apply supported prototype background effort without changing the model.
  [ ] Qualify profile settings and timing/accuracy budgets against real hardware.
  [x] Record complete-slice authority and all-feature implementation/limits matrix.

Acceptance criteria:
  The product brief distinguishes approved decisions from proposals.
  One first-slice workflow has observable success and failure outcomes.
  Agent access and side effects require explicit authority and visible controls.
  Stack and integration decisions include rationale and verified interface evidence.
  No real private-data access or agent execution is needed merely to define scope.
  Scope remains explicit: the owner authorized an early fixture-only prototype;
  roadmap items still do not authorize continuous microphone/provider/agent
  execution. Later explicit selection/check and prototype-release authority
  does not waive those boundaries.
  The later bounded speech request explicitly permits installed local-English
  push-to-talk and conversation-only dispatch to one existing idle session,
  not general work tools, active attach, cloud audio or always listening.
  The subsequent complete-slice request permits actual mesh reads at every
  launch and confirmed work in existing idle sessions, retaining permissions
  and refusing busy targets. Push-to-talk is selected for this prototype.
  Default performance profile and selectable priorities preserve target/permission
  correctness; latency results are not substituted for processing accuracy.

Completion evidence:
  User selected architecture/roadmap only, wake-word activation, existing Agency
  sessions first, and local/cloud speech research before selection.
  docs/architecture.md records proposed components, telemetry and outcome semantics.
  docs/speech-options.md compares sourced candidates without claiming voice tests.
  docs/roadmap.md defines phase gates and the first end-to-end scenario.
  CareerOS graph source and installed Agency/Copilot help inspected read-only.
  User subsequently confirmed performance/speed first and default, with options
  to select priorities. Accuracy remains important; actual inference/speech
  profile settings remain unqualified.
  The owner subsequently authorized a fixture prototype and approved Electron
  for it. At that milestone no final stack/provider or live connector proof existed.
  Deeper research recommends Tauri 2/Rust + React/TypeScript/Three.js,
  native sherpa audio and SQLite; Electron is retained as a fallback.
  docs/technology-decisions.md and docs/research-sources.md explain rationale
  and evidence limits. docs/runtime-contracts.md and
  docs/performance-and-qualification.md define proposed behavior and gates.
  Windows endpoint AEC, resumed permissions and actual existing-session control
  remain explicit qualification risks, not completed proofs.
  docs/prototype.md preserves that initial synthetic desktop milestone,
  transparent Floating Mesh and rendering profiles as historical evidence.
  The owner additionally requested an EXE prerelease and microphone selection.
  An unsigned Windows x64 portable prerelease and saved input picker now exist;
  device checks do not select a speech provider or implement speech recognition.
  Subsequently selected installed Windows en-US recognition and English local
  playback, click-on/click-off input and one dedicated existing idle Copilot
  session for the conversation prototype. Broader product/provider decisions
  and performance/hardware/multilingual qualification remain open.
  The later .4 prototype uses actual mesh startup, explicit session/task
  confirmation and manual-policy ACP background work. The native source
  acceptance test executed the exact approved Get-Location task and returned
  "Hello world." with completed local playback. Saved grants meant no native
  permission prompt; physical voice, human listening and speed SLOs remain
  unqualified. Existing model retained, supported background effort maps
  Speed/Balanced/Accuracy to low/medium/high. No automatic terminal dispatch,
  active attach, durable queue/outbox or full-duplex claim. The current feature
  matrix distinguishes coverage, implementation and qualified/deferred behavior.

